insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/001', 'LOC', 'Żerań (OP-Żerań, Z1)', 'The hub of the survivor community, on the Vistula at the north of the corridor.', 'The hub. Power, radio and water access are routed from here. Class D ground. Grid reference 52.2926 N, 20.9964 E. A radio built from boards, scavenged parts and hand-wound coils stands here, with cable run up the stairwell toward the chimney, and it is among the first things Diana saw there. Janek Dąbrowski sat beside Goya Liszka on the dock at Żerań, early in Goya''s time in the zone, and listened to him curse for ten minutes. The abandoned houseboat on which Diana Antonoff lived is moored at Żerań, at the northern edge of the wetlands. The staff gauge on the Żerań dock is read daily at 07:00. The canal is open in summer, with lily pads, reed and a beaver, and frozen at the margins in winter, with ice 11 centimetres thick by 9 December 2034. Diana counted dragonflies of four species at the dock on thirty-one mornings. The Russian post RM, at Tarchomin, lies about three kilometres from the dock and presses directly on the hub. The Dendron was measured in the cooling channel, where a mature mass lowered the water by 11 centimetres in nine days, and it does not grow on the bricks of the power station. The hub was destroyed in the third flood. The lights failed at 02:05 on 1 January 2035, the last in the corridor to do so, and radio contact ended at 03:05 in the middle of a sentence. The occupier watched from the Citadel, with a searchlight, for two and a half hours. The red and white chimney was not brought down. The number of persons at Żerań that night is not recorded.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/002', 'LOC', 'Zoo (OP-Zoo, Z2)', 'A community gathering place with large grounds and good cover.', 'Community gathering. Large grounds, good cover. Class D ground. Grid reference 52.2573 N, 21.0255 E. About two hundred and ten people live in the Zoo, in enclosures that have been made into houses. The lion house is the largest. It has a barred front, which serves as the wall of a stove, and a long hall where the cages were. At about six each evening the people come in from the cold and sit on the floor. The keeper''s house is also used. The Dendron reached the lower Zoo in the second flood. A woman at the Zoo leaves a saucer of milk at the foot of the largest mass each morning at seven. The woman at the Zoo is named in Protocol 003 as Mrs Kaczmarek, who believes the Dendron was sent to teach patience. She has been at stage 1 for three months. On 17 November 2034 Diana counted about sixty in the lion house, nineteen of them children, when Tanja Dopolewka played the violin from an upturned feed bin with her back to the old viewing window. Three of Catalina''s stage 2 patients wept. The violin carries across the water to the Docks, about two kilometres, when the air is still. Statements from the western powers are read aloud at the Zoo in the evenings. The keeper''s house stands at the south end, inside the old reptile garden. It is a small brick house with a pitched roof and a tin chimney, and it belongs to Pan and Pani Wróbel, who have taken in two children, Anna and Nikita. Visitors knock twice and wait, because Nikita is frightened of a single knock.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/003', 'LOC', 'Brodno (OP-Brodno, SZ)', 'The old hospital, which the survivors use as their medical post.', 'Szpital (Brodno). Medical. Defensible. Multiple buildings. Class D ground. Grid reference 52.2984 N, 21.1071 E. Catalina Chmielewska treats patients with documented exposure here, and she allowed a visitor to sit in the ward at a distance of two metres. A chapel stands on the grounds. Alex''s note in Chapter 8: The Medic puts Diana''s meeting with Catalina here in the second week of October 2034, and her diary of 11 October 2034 is set in the ward. Catalina''s data record 3 of 81 persons exposed at stage 3 or beyond who did not become compliant. All three, on the first day of exposure, were doing something for someone else. Diana''s last notes place the three at Brodno. The hospital stands on a low rise and has kept its feet dry. It is a long building in grey brick with four wings and a chapel. The ground floor and the first floor are wards, and every room that is not a ward is a store. Water is carried from a cistern in the yard. In the dispensary a ledger records the stock of each drug and, beside it, the number of people who will not receive it. Pawel keeps the stores. The Brigade has held a hearing in the chapel, with thirty people on the pews. A scientist who had worked eleven years in the laboratory sat on the chapel step after his hour in the ward. Alisia Lewandowska stood at the door at night in the autumn of 2034, when her mother had pneumonia, and did not knock. The field map gives Brodno and Siekierki the same code, S1. The register uses SZ for Brodno.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/004', 'LOC', 'Praski (OP-P1)', 'The market of the corridor, where trade and exchange take place.', 'Praski. Market, trade, exchange. Class D ground. Grid reference 52.2483 N, 21.0310 E. The older project note called this place the Market. Alisia Lewandowska lists a man at Praski with a stick among those who have looked at her. She also took a tin of peaches from a man at Praski who kept a stall and whom she knew to be a crook, and left it at night on Catalina Chmielewska''s dispensary shelf. The manuscript gives no other description of the post.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/005', 'LOC', 'Rynek, Grochów (OP-Rynek, R1)', 'A gathering point in the middle of the corridor.', 'Rynek (Grochów). Gathering point. Mid-corridor. Class D ground. Grid reference 52.2553 N, 21.0491 E. A diary entry is set in a room above the pharmacy. The room above the pharmacy is a long narrow loft with a sloping ceiling. A table along one wall holds five radios: two military, one shortwave, one field set Diana did not know, and one kitchen radio with a cracked case. Headphones hang on a hook, a small lamp with a green shade stands on the table, and a notebook is ruled into six columns, the sixth without a heading. Magda Wiśniewska listens here. At 23:00 the posts R0, RM, RW, RN and R4 check in, five flat voices in Russian. A woman who runs the Rynek kitchen is mentioned, and Diana comes to Rynek to ask Magda about the Kontakt rota. On a night in November Magda told Zoysa Zieliński here that a man named on the Szucha net as a consultant, with the rank of colonel, spoke with the accent of Rembertów.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/006', 'LOC', 'Gocławek (OP-G1)', 'The eastern edge of the corridor, exposed, and of high value for intelligence.', 'Gocławek. Eastern edge. Exposed. Intelligence value high. Class C ground. Grid reference 52.2357 N, 21.1246 E. Class C ground is defined in Protocol 001 as occasionally waterlogged and unpredictable. The register is the only passage in the manuscript that names Gocławek. It does not say who holds the post or what happens there.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/007', 'LOC', 'Mała Afryka (OP-A1)', 'A river access point on which the corridor depends for supply.', 'Mała Afryka. River access. Supply. Class D ground. Grid reference 52.2234 N, 21.0660 E. Class D ground is dry but cut off, and is used for survivor routes and bases. The register is the only passage in the manuscript that names Mała Afryka. It does not say what is carried through the post, or by whom.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/008', 'LOC', 'Czerniaków (OP-Czerniaków, C1)', 'A river outpost in the south, with the escape route running out of it.', 'Czerniaków. River access. Escape route south. Class D ground. Grid reference 52.2221 N, 21.0478 E. The embankment is part of the outpost. Diana Antonoff was last seen here, on the night of the third flood. A document was compiled here between 23:50 on 31 December 2034 and 05:40 on 1 January 2035. On 23 October a boy pulled a crate from the water at Czerniaków which carried a label in a western language. Protocol 005 was compiled at the outpost, with a radio, a recorder, a notebook and a candle, fifteen kilometres south of Żerań. Kontakt point KC, at Mokotów and Czerniaków, watches the south near the flood margin. Diana was asked to go because a Kontakt point there had been silent for two days, and she set out on 31 December 2034. Persons crossing from Czerniaków and Wilanów are received at the Służewiec racecourse. On the embankment before dawn on 1 January 2035 it was minus 12 degrees and still, with a red glow in the north and the river ice turning from grey to pale rose. An engine without lights came slowly down the channel from the north. A Runner of about fifteen, Oliwia, left with the pouch of Diana''s last pages by a culvert two hundred metres from the embankment. Diana''s last diary entry breaks off in the middle of a sentence.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/009', 'LOC', 'Siekierki (OP-SK)', 'An exposed industrial position on the southern riverside.', 'Siekierki. Southern riverside. Industrial. Exposed. Class B ground. Grid reference 52.2072 N, 21.0870 E. Class B ground is defined in Protocol 001 as shallow, seasonal flood, passable with knowledge. The field map gives Siekierki and Szpital (Brodno) the same code, S1. The register resolves this by using SK for Siekierki and SZ for Brodno, and notes that the field map has not been corrected. No scene is set at Siekierki.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/010', 'LOC', 'Las (OP-L1)', 'A concealed forest position that the Runners use as a waypoint.', 'Las. Forest. Concealed. Runner waypoint. Class D ground. Grid reference 52.1982 N, 21.1145 E. Class D ground is dry but cut off, and is used for survivor routes and bases. The register is the only passage in the manuscript that names Las. It does not say which Runners use it or on what routes.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/011', 'LOC', 'Przyloty (OP-P2)', 'A position deep in the margin that can be reached only in some seasons.', 'Przyloty. Deep in the margin. Accessibility seasonal. Class B ground. Grid reference 52.1456 N, 21.1711 E. Protocol 001 defines Class B as shallow, seasonal flood, passable with knowledge. The register gives Przyloty no function beyond its position. The same document describes the corridor as a chain of dry places joined by shallow margins that can be crossed by boat or on foot if the crossing is known, and states the rule that governs this position: what is passable in June is not passable in January. The readings for each season are provisional. No other passage in the manuscript names Przyloty, and no character is recorded as going there.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/012', 'LOC', 'Pałac, Wilanów (OP-P3)', 'The outpost at the southern end of the corridor, in large grounds, and a link in the escape route.', 'Pałac (Wilanów). Southern end of the corridor. Grounds large. Escape route link. Class D ground. Grid reference 52.1653 N, 21.0897 E. It is not the Palace of Culture and Science in the centre of the city. Class D ground is dry but cut off, and serves as survivor route and base. Protocol 001 names Wilanów as the southern end of the corridor. The escape route ZAC begins here and runs south past the palace grounds, through Bielawa, toward Konstancin-Jeziorna and then east. It is about 50 km, the longest of the four routes, rural once clear of the city, and is used when the others are compromised. It takes two nights with a day lying up, 16 to 20 hours moving. In summer it is affected by southern flood levels, and its winter condition has not been recorded. The Kontakt point KP at Wilanów is listed as southern and near this outpost. Protocol 002 places the occupier''s southern exit block, RKS, south of Wilanów, and says that persons who cross from Wilanów are taken to the Służewiec racecourse (estimated). In the Prologue, Alex is travelling south on the ZAC route when he meets Diana Antonoff at a farm in the grey zone.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/013', 'LOC', 'Filtry (OP-Filtry, F1)', 'The water filtration station, and the only reliably clean supply in the corridor.', 'Filtry. Water filtration. Critical. Near Russian R0. Class D ground. Grid reference 52.2206 N, 21.0080 E. It is the survivor position closest to the western ring. The supply is treated with charcoal and a three-stage sand bed, and it is the only supply in the corridor that is reliably free of the compound. The post at R0 has it in sight. Protocol 002 places R0 in Ochota, on the west bank and adjacent to the station, and says that every litre reaching the corridor passes under its eye. The register also lists a forward Kontakt point, KF, central and near the station, marked dangerous because it is close to the west bank. The Diana first records that the filtration is the reason the occupier has not taken the station, and then withdraws the statement: the occupier has left it alone because it is not worth taking, and the relationship runs the other way round.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/014', 'LOC', 'Termika (OP-Termika, the Docks)', 'The dock outpost where the water levels of the corridor are measured.', 'The engineer at Termika, Lucasz Kowalski, keeps a table of water levels by day. It is the only reliable series in the corridor. The staff gauge series covers the Termika and Żerań docks. Two diary entries are set at the Docks, dated 26 July 2034 and 2 September 2034. In the second flood the Dendron appeared at the Docks, where it had not been recorded before. In one flood the Docks became an island, and Lucasz stood at the main sluice gate. The control room is a concrete cube with a steel door, built as a pumping station and put to other work. It holds valves, handwheels and a hand-drawn schematic with a newer one beneath it, corrected in red. On 26 July 2034, with the staff gauge at 84.1 m, Diana Antonoff followed Lucasz through a day of inspection, and at evening a violin carried from the Zoo, about two kilometres to the south across the water. He stopped work until it ended. In the second flood the Docks overtopped at 02:50 on 2 September 2034, and the lower quay was gone by 03:15. Lucasz held the gate from 01:00 and opened the second gate at 03:40. Three people were lost: Wacław Pietrzak, 71, who kept the ferry; Iga, 23, a Runner; and a man whose name was not taken. On the night of 30 October a Russian biochemist came to the Docks and asked for the engineer.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/015', 'LOC', 'The Citadel', 'The occupier''s command centre, in the north of the city.', 'The nineteenth-century fortress on the Vistula in the north of the city. Command centre. It controls river access from the north. Colonel Viktor Nikolaevich Sorokin, the commanding officer, is based here. Protocol 002 records that no excesses are attributed to him, and that a source who delivered food to his office in July found the desk clear but for a single paperback and a glass of water. At sunset each day a searchlight is turned from the Citadel to the river and passes once across the east bank, slowly, for forty seconds, and goes dark. Protocol 002 rates it the most reliable event in the corridor and calls it a statement. Alex later writes that people remember the occupation as a light and not as an event. On the night of 31 December the beam was not swept but held on Żerąń for two and a half hours from 02:30. Protocol 005 records that the mechanism of the third flood needed a sluice that could be opened from the Citadel, and that it had been prepared for a long time.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/016', 'LOC', 'The Palace of Culture and Science', 'A gift from Moscow, now a supply hub and the source of the broadcasts.', 'A gift from Moscow in 1955, designed to be seen from every part of the city. It is seen from every part of the city. It is now a logistics and supply hub, and it carries a mast from which broadcasts are made, in Russian and in Polish. The loudspeaker transcript of Magda Wiśniewska comes from these broadcasts. The broadcast studio is in the Palace. Studio Two is on the eighth floor, and the announcer is kept in a room on the nineteenth with a lock on the outside and a view over the roofs of Śródmieście. The corridors carry a carpet in red and gold laid in 1955. Protocol 002 gives the loudspeaker at 08:00 and 20:00, from the Palace and the Old Town together. Interlude IV gives the script four readings a day, at 08:00, 12:00, 18:00 and 20:00, with a shorter form on the half hour at night, over a network of two thousand speakers. The headnote to Interlude I calls the transcript the Palace loudspeaker, and Wiktor Szeliga hears it from Block C, two and a half kilometres away. An informant also passed the Brigade the state of the fuel stocks at the Palace in winter.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/017', 'LOC', 'The Old Town and the Royal Castle', 'A rebuilt district, occupied for its symbolism.', 'Rebuilt after 1944 from photographs and paintings. Occupied for its symbolism. Loudspeakers stand on the ramparts. Protocol 001 lists the Old Town among the districts of the west bank, which stands on higher ground and is drier and occupied. Protocol 002 gives the loudspeaker at 08:00 and 20:00 from the Old Town and the Palace together. The broadcasts speak of order, restored services and warm beds, in a woman''s pleasant voice, and do not mention the water. In Protocol 005 Diana returns to the rebuilding: the city was rebuilt from photographs and paintings after 1944, Warsaw is proud of it, and Fritha Cielinski has several thousand photographs. Diana makes no further comment.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/018', 'LOC', 'The Saxon Garden and Łazienki', 'Open ground that the occupier has turned to air defence.', 'Open ground turned to air defence. Missile batteries have been seen between the trees. Protocol 002 counts it among five places of consequence on the west bank, which is drier than the east and held by the occupier with ease. The report is a single sighting. It does not say which of the two parks holds the batteries, or how many, and no other passage in the manuscript names either park. It is the only air defence recorded in the manuscript.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/019', 'LOC', 'Szucha and Pawiak', 'Two buildings of interrogation and imprisonment that the occupier has taken over unchanged.', 'The German security police took the building at Aleja Szucha 25 in 1939 and used its cellars for interrogation. The Russian force has reoccupied it. A tunnel is reported to run from the cellars to the old prison at Pawiak, which has been reactivated. Persons are taken in. Some are returned. A source who walked past Pawiak on 14 September counted seven lit windows. In each case the occupier has built nothing. Szucha was built to be afraid of. Pawiak was built to hold. Major Alexei Dmitrievich Budanov, head of screening and intelligence, operates from Szucha, and his name is on the orders. Interlude III is set in the building. The bedroom is on the third floor of the north wing and the office is room 214, with a plaster rose on the ceiling. A courtyard has a plane tree. The screening room is on the lower ground floor, was a cell, and has a drain in its rubber floor. Screening runs in seven steps and ends in one of four decisions: release, retain, transfer or treat. In Chapter 10 Magda Wiśniewska says that she gave the occupier eleven names as a child, that four of those people were taken to Szucha, and that two returned. Szucha is also the name of a radio net that Magda monitors. Pawiak is named in no passage other than Protocol 002.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/020', 'LOC', 'R0 and RM', 'Two Russian posts on the water, one of which watches the filtration station.', 'RM stands about three kilometres from the Żerań dock and can be seen from the roof of the control room on a clear morning. It has not fired. R0 is the post that matters to the water. The filtration station at Filtry stands within sight of it, and every litre that reaches the corridor passes under its eye. R0 is also the call sign under which the rota is read on the observation net. From the observation post a rota is read aloud every sixth day, at 06:00, to six field commanders, who each reply Received and a number. R0 stands in Ochota, on the west bank, adjacent to Filtry. RM stands at Tarchomin, north of Żerąń, and puts direct pressure on the hub; its coordinates are given as approximate. Protocol 002 records a radio check of all posts every four hours, on the hour. On 22 September 2034 Magda Wiśniewska let Diana hear five posts check in at 23:00: R0, RM, RW, RN and R4. On the night of 31 December, Kontakt KB reported many engines to the north, at RM. Interlude II is set in the observation post: a room four metres by three, in cinder block, with a steel shutter raised twenty centimetres over a strip of the city. Through it Sasha Lebedev sees an eleven-storey block whose lower four floors are covered by green growth advancing four to nine centimetres a day. He had been at the post ninety-four days on 4 October and was relieved on 11 October. The survivors call him the Watcher.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/021', 'LOC', 'SP-1, Marymont', 'A screening point in the north, in a sports academy.', 'The sports halls and halls of residence of the physical education academy on Marymoncka, on the left bank opposite Białołęka. It receives persons who cross in the north. Two buses a day come along the Marymoncka road, one at about 07:00 and one at about 16:00. They arrive with the blinds down and leave with the blinds up. What is done in the halls is not known. The lights are on. Protocol 002 gives its position as approximately 52.272 N, 20.962 E. It is one of at least four points west of the Vistula, each marked SP in black stencil on the leaflets and on the buses. The bus times are the count of one Runner who works the northern reach, and the Protocol marks them as estimated. The compiler had no source who had been through a screening point and returned willing to say what is done there. The observations were made from the east bank between 3 September and 20 October 2034.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/022', 'LOC', 'SP-2, Torwar', 'A screening point in an ice arena at the centre.', 'The ice arena and sports hall on Łazienkowska, near the river, in the centre. It receives persons who cross at the central bridges. The lights are on all night. The ice is said to be still kept frozen, and the power for it was the first restored in that district. That is a large use of power for a place that offers warm beds. Protocol 002 gives its position as approximately 52.216 N, 21.040 E. The report of the ice and the power comes from one source, who worked the south shore before the second flood. Interlude III: Major Budanov, Aleja Szucha, describes a checkpoint on the Poniatowski Bridge, where arrivals are assessed on the bridge and passed on to a reception hall on the left bank. The manuscript does not say whether that hall is this arena.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/023', 'LOC', 'SP-3, Służewiec', 'A screening point at the racecourse, the only one with its own fence.', 'The racecourse in Mokotów: stables, a grandstand and a very large open ground. It receives persons who cross from Czerniaków and Wilanów, in the south. It is the only point with a fence of its own. The fence is new, two and a half metres high, in pale galvanised wire that shows against the grass in photographs taken from the flood margin. Persons are queued on the open ground, and the queue is said to be orderly. Protocol 002 gives its position as approximately 52.185 N, 20.970 E. Mokotów is on the west bank, on the higher and drier ground. Of the three points the compiler can place, it is the only one for which Protocol 002 cites photographs.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/024', 'LOC', 'SP-4', 'A fourth screening point, known by its number only.', 'The location of SP-4 is not known. It is known by its number only, from a bus destination board seen on the embankment road. Protocol 002 states that there are at least four screening points, all west of the Vistula, and gives this sighting as the reason it says at least four. Its table lists the point as not placed, with the crossings it receives from and its coordinates both not known.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/025', 'LOC', 'Okęcie', 'The airport, a supply hub and the subject of a rumour.', 'The airport is not a screening point. It is the supply hub, and it is where the aircraft come. Among survivors it is believed that certain persons are not sent on to the shelters at all but are flown out from Okęcie, and that they are the persons the occupier most wishes to keep. The word used is rendered. The belief has been heard in three places that do not speak to one another. Protocol 002 also lists the airport among the posts of the outer ring as RA, Chopin Airport, with the purpose supply hub and air control, at approximately 52.166 N, 20.967 E. It uses RA for the airport and RKS for the post south of Wilanów, to resolve two codes the field map gives twice. In Protocol 001 no route west can be made, because of the airport and the occupied west bank, and three attempts were abandoned. In Protocol 004, link WT-05 of the evidence chain is the route of a single supply flight into the airport, flown three times at night with no declared cargo, and is rated 0.6.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/026', 'LOC', 'The shelters', 'Numbered places in the west where those screened are said to be sent.', 'The shelters are numbered. Persons who pass a screening point are sent on to them. Shelters 1 to 6 were heard of in 2034. The numbers rise: Interlude VI, in June 2035, is set in Shelter 9, a former sports hall on the left bank. The offer of them is made every day, by loudspeaker and by leaflet. The leaflet is a single A5 sheet with a photograph of a bed, and the voice says that the shelters on the left bank are open and that there are beds. Protocol 002 counts about 340 persons as having accepted the offer since June 2034. Shelter 9 is a hall forty-two metres by twenty-four, with 260 camp beds in rows of ten, each with a numbered grey blanket. The washroom is the old changing room, with twenty-four basins. The loudspeaker is on the wall above the double doors and is much louder than on the east bank. The warden, Pan Cichocki, a retired surveyor, keeps an intake ledger that sorts each person into one of five categories. The lights go down to a third at 22:00. A hum from the vent below the scoreboard, once in six seconds, is noted once in the ledger margin. Alex marks in the text which details he has supplied himself. He has asked in four shelters and does not know what became of Wiktor Szeliga.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/027', 'LOC', 'Escape routes', 'The gaps in the occupier''s ring through which survivors leave.', 'The outer ring is not a wall. It is a series of eyes with gaps between them, and the gaps are the escape routes. No post is held in strength greater than a company. The occupier knows where the gaps are, and Diana Antonoff believes it has left them open because it prefers to watch what passes through. The route from Czerniaków runs south, and the palace at Wilanów is a link in it. The most direct route passes close to a Russian post and is high risk. In winter, when the water is frozen, there is a winter route, which the Runners use. Protocol 001, section 8, tabulates four routes. The north route, the most direct and the preferred route over ice in winter, is 25 km, through the gap between the posts RN and R2, past the Kontakt point KB toward Nieporęt. It takes one night on foot, seven to nine hours, or about four hours across the ice in deep winter, and in summer it is high risk. The north-east route passes wide of the post at Kobyłka toward Pustelnik, 45 km, with woodland cover, and is called the safest and the one used to move high-value persons. The eastern route, 30 km past the Kontakt points KJ and KW and south through Józefów, is the everyday route and the most monitored. The southern route runs 50 km from the palace at Wilanów through Bielawa toward Konstancin-Jeziorna, and is used when the others are compromised. No route leads west, because of the airport and the occupied west bank.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/028', 'LOC', 'The sewers', 'The tunnels under the city, used as a way through in 1944 and again now.', 'The sewers are a way through the city. In August 1944 a unit of the Home Army moved through the sewers under Śródmieście. In the present account Anna carried the child Nikita down to the sewers, because that was where one went, and the two travelled through them for three weeks. Nikita, at six, was the best ear in the tunnels. The Dendron has been found in the sewers of Targówek. A mass in a sewer does not remove its function but blocks it, so that the drains back up and the water rises. The account of 1944 is a story that Marek Olszewski tells at the kitchen table on Thursdays, which Diana Antonoff records in her diary of 30 November 2034. The unit''s captain held his line for three weeks, and the men later found in his coat a note offering him terms. The account of the three weeks is Nikita''s own, written on a pad in Wrocław in February 2036, when she is eight. Anna was thirteen. In the dark Nikita could hear the water in the pipe, people breathing far off and boots overhead, and she signalled to Anna with her fingers on Anna''s wrist. She also heard a low sound in the pipes, like a person crying with a hand over the mouth, and it did not frighten her.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/029', 'LOC', 'St Michael''s Cathedral', 'A flooded cathedral on the Praga shore.', 'The cathedral stands on the Praga side of the water and the water has been in it. A tide line runs across the nave pillars at the height of a hip. The roof is open in two places over the north aisle. Pigeons nest there, and there is a smell of wet plaster and old incense. The west door hangs from one hinge. The diary entry is dated 9 August 2034, at 06:15, under overcast and low light, with the water flat and the colour of pewter. Diana stands for a full minute inside the west door to let her eyes find the dark. The nave holds several thousand photographs, laid in rows on the altar steps and along the lowest courses of the pews, each weighted at the corners with a pebble or a spent cartridge, and more are pegged on lines between the pillars. They are sorted by year: weddings in one aisle, holidays in another, school photographs in a long rank. Felicyta Cielinski, nineteen, sits among them on a folding stool and talks to the prints. A child of about six, Nikita, sits on the floor and touches none. A Runner stays at the door and will not come in. In her own testimony Fritha calls the place the church, gives the count as five thousand, and says she brought the prints in by boat and laid them on the steps. Alex notes in Chapter 3 that Fritha''s church is in Class D, under the name of a place that was a cathedral.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/030', 'LOC', 'The wetlands and the houseboat', 'The still water at the north of the corridor where Diana lived.', 'Diana Antonoff lived alone on an abandoned houseboat moored at Żerań, at the northern edge of the wetlands, where the water ran quietest. Her diary entries are headed with the date, the place, the weather and the state of the water. Many are set on the deck and in the cabin of the houseboat. The houseboat is an hour or more on foot from the outpost, by way of the one bridge the community holds, though it can be seen across the water. It lies low on the near bank among willow, reed and broad pale leaves, and a passer-by at twenty metres would not know a hull was there. One other boat is moored further along. From the deck the red and white chimneys of the power plant stand across the canal, and at night the hum of the plant carries over. When Diana first goes aboard on 1 June 2034 the cabin has a cup on its side, a calendar left on a month long past, and a folding settee with a worn cover. The canal in summer has reeds, lily pads, a beaver and dragonflies, which Protocol 001 records in its notes. On 9 December the canal ice is walkable at the margin, and by 28 December it is frozen from bank to bank. Diana''s last entries are written from the deck on 30 December 2034. The Foreword records that she disappeared from Czerniaków on the night of the third flood, and the houseboat could not be reached afterwards.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOC/031', 'LOC', 'Żerań power station', 'A power station on which the Dendron does not grow.', 'The Dendron does not grow on the bricks of the Żerań power station. Diana Antonoff, who compiled Protocol 003, has no explanation and examined it on four days. The organism grows up to the edge of the old coal yard and stops. It is a note to file, not a finding. From the houseboat across the canal the chimneys of the plant are striped red and white, and the hum of the plant carries at night. The log in Protocol 005 records that the station basement flooded and the lights at Żerań went out at 02:05 on 1 January 2035, and that eleven rounds were fired at 02:40 at the chimney, the control room and the dock. Radio contact with the Żerań station ended at 03:05 in the middle of the sentence "Tell Diana the chimney is". Alex later finds the red and white stack standing, the only thing at Żerań not brought down, with reeds grown up around it and a heron at its foot, and does not know why it was spared.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/001', 'CHR', 'Diana Antonoff', 'Ukrainian journalist, aged 28, who documented the survivor community and assembled the Protocols. Disappeared on the night of the third flood.', 'Diana Antonoff was a Ukrainian journalist who filed dispatches to Suspilne. She lived alone on an abandoned houseboat moored at Żerań, at the northern edge of the wetlands, where the others said the water ran quietest. She disappeared from the survivor outpost at Czerniaków on the night of the third flood, in the first January of the occupation''s seventh year. She was twenty-eight. Her last dispatch was filed the Tuesday before. No body was recovered and no statement was issued. The community she had documented entered her name among the missing and continued the work she had taught them. She is the source of the Codex marks and of the recorded testimonies. A nine-minute tape of her voice survives and is used once in the archive. She entered the corridor on 1 June 2034, by reed boat before first light, having crossed the grey zone alone. Alex met her once, for half an hour, at a farm south of the city shortly before she went in, where she ate energy gels from a sachet and carried a brick of camera batteries wrapped in cloth. Interlude I describes her at twenty-eight as small and dark, her Polish careful and foreign. She opened every diary page in the same order: date, place, conditions, temperature, pressure and flood level. She wrote her notes against each transcript in pencil. She compiled all five Protocols. Her last diary entry, written at OP-Czerniaków on 1 January 2035 under the heading of the third flood, stops in mid-sentence. In it she gives the pouch of final pages to a Runner, tells her that she will follow, and records that this was untrue. The safe on the houseboat held a camera, a spare battery, the key to the boat and a small framed photograph whose description matches the print that Fritha took of her. The manuscript does not say whether she lived. The Foreword says only that if she is alive, she has chosen not to be found.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/002', 'CHR', 'Goya Liszka', 'Veteran of Mariupol and the Runners'' guide through the wetlands. Swears without pause. Hears drones before anyone else.', 'Goya Liszka served in the last cellar at Mariupol, under what had been a bakery. Ten men held it. Nine died when a shell came through. He was at the back of the room and lay three days among the dead because to move was to be heard. No camp or column would take him afterwards, because he does not sleep and watches the door. The Zone took him because it asked only what he could do. He can hear a drone in the air before anyone lifts a head, and he reads a man''s route from the mud on his boots. He knows where every Runner in the wetlands is at any hour, and which chimneys at Żerań still carry a watcher. He swears at everything: the boat, the sky, the dead, God, his own hands. Diana records him as the most damaged person she had met in the Zone and the most necessary. He speaks to children and drones in the same voice. In the ward at Brodno he called Pawel "the banker" and told him to count a Runner''s blood on the floor, and was told by Catalina that he was bleeding on her floor, which he was. He stood at the door when Lucasz was locked in the control room. In 2036 he lives at a veterans'' facility outside Gdańsk, a former sanatorium with long glassed corridors, and sits in every room with his back to the wall. Alex reached him only by saying that he had been to see Janek, at which the swearing stopped. On the dock at Żerań Goya cursed God for ten minutes in front of Janek, who called it an honest prayer, and the two have sat together since. On the night of the second flood, 2 September 2034, he had the Runners out by 01:45, twenty-three children in four boats, and all twenty-three came back. He says that in all the years none of his has been taken. He took Zoysa to the range when she arrived at fifteen, and did not speak to her for four days after she told him what she had kept from him. Diana noted that he did not swear at Janek.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/003', 'CHR', 'Felicyta "Fritha" Cielinski', 'Aged 19. Keeps photographs. Found in a church in Class D ground.', 'Fritha Cielinski was nineteen when Diana met her. A Runner at the door, who would not come in, gave her name and said only that the girl in the church was not to be hurried. Her church stands in Class D ground, under the name of a place that was a cathedral. She keeps photographs. Forty-one prints came out of the water in her father''s jacket, which she found on a railing six days afterwards, caught by the sleeve. She dried them on a radiator in a stairwell without power. The emulsion had lifted and her mother''s face had slid a little to the left. Her account is that photographs do not keep people. They keep a version of the light that fell on someone, and a person who has made a photograph into a god has nothing else to pray to. She was nineteen when Diana told her that a photograph is a promise to remember and not the memory itself, and she thought it the most unfair thing anyone had said to her. She still thinks so, but she looks at people while they are in the room. Alex met her in Berlin in November 2035, when she was twenty or twenty-one. She works in the basement of a museum, in a long room kept at eighteen degrees, restoring recordings and glass negatives in white cotton gloves, and she would not be photographed. In a plan chest drawer she keeps found prints in glassine sleeves, at least a hundred and ninety, each row marked Unclaimed, and says that in two years no one has looked. In the church at Praga the prints lay in rows on the altar steps and hung from lines between the pillars, sorted by year and weighted with pebbles or spent cartridges, and she spoke to them as to people. Diana visited every second day for three weeks, and on the twenty-second day Fritha agreed to photograph her. She kept the negative. The framed print in Diana''s safe, of a woman on a shoreline looking at the water, matches that photograph. Nikita stayed with her through most of that autumn, and Fritha gave her the one print she never boxed.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/004', 'CHR', 'Janek Dąbrowski', 'Wanderer. Lodged with Rosa and Marek. Spiritual anchor of the community.', 'Janek Dąbrowski came to rest in October. In September Rosa Olszewska offered him the empty back room of the third-floor flat in Białołęka that she shared with Marek. The room had a window, a bed with a real mattress, a wool blanket and a stove, and had been empty since the winter. His account of the offer is that it is not a sin to want to be kept, and that the rule, if there is one, is not that a person must not want but that a person must not want what belongs to someone else and call it theirs. The room was Rosa''s to give, not to keep. He was fifty-five in August 2034 and fifty-seven when Alex met him. He studied for the priesthood and left it to marry, and his wife died of cancer five years before the invasion. He has owned since then a coat, a pair of boots and a prayer book. Diana met him on 19 August 2034 on the dock at Żerań, sitting beside Goya in a silence that the two took turns to keep. He asked her nothing, and she said aloud that she had not prayed in two years. He told her that the city is in God, and not the other way about. It was Diana who asked him, in the doorway of the room, whether he could not have it or did not think he was allowed. His wife was Hanna, and they were married for twenty-three years in a small flat in Praga with a green table that had one short leg and a folded beer mat under it. In January 2036 Alex found him at a refugee centre outside Przemyśl, a converted barracks, where he makes tea and at four each afternoon walks a round of the doors with a steel flask and four paper cups. He keeps a room two doors from the kitchen for anyone who cannot sleep near others breathing, and strips the bed in the morning. He says that he still believes, in a smaller form than before.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/005', 'CHR', 'Lucasz Kowalski', 'Engineer who runs the control room. Locked in it for seven hours by his friends.', 'Lucasz Kowalski is the engineer of the community''s systems. He reads the gauge at the dock and keeps the control room. Artur told Tanja that the engineer had a fault and that she was the only thing that could fix it. On a Thursday in late October a group arranged for Lucasz to be locked in the control room: Artur took the key from his belt, Goya stood at the door with folded arms and told him to sit down, and Diana neither took part nor stopped it. The door did not open for seven hours. On the night of the pressure fall his reading of the dock gauge at 18:00, up fourteen centimetres on the morning, was relayed to Diana by Artur, who ran. Diana''s diary of 26 July 2034 places him at OP-Termika, the Docks: a man of about thirty-six in a canvas jacket gone white at the seams, who laid the flat of his hand on forty-three objects in one day before he spoke a figure. The control room is a concrete cube with valves, handwheels and a schematic corrected in red. Before the war he was a civil engineer on the Vistula Canal, a relief scheme. He wrote its gate sequence, and the occupiers took the control room and ran it in reverse, so that the canal brought the water into the city. When the violin sounded from the Zoo he stopped where he stood. On 2 September 2034 he stood at the main gate from 01:00 and opened the second gate at 03:40. At 01:20 on 1 January 2035 he sent his last transmission from Żerań: the occupiers had swapped the same two lines. In December 2035 Alex found him on a sluice site on the Martwa Wisła outside Gdańsk, where the work stops at four on Fridays and he takes off his hat.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/006', 'CHR', 'Tanja Dopolewka', 'Aged 36. Violinist who teaches a mixed class of survivors.', 'Tanja Dopolewka was thirty-six, small and square-shouldered, in a man''s cardigan. By every account the archivist was given she had been one of the best violinists in Warsaw. She ran a room on the second floor with a piano, a carpet and a row of small plastic chairs. On the Saturday of the visit eight people were present, aged between nine and seventy, each holding an instrument. She taught with the words "Again" and "Listen to her, not to yourself". When a boy of eleven dropped his bow, a black carbon-fibre bow with white hair, she went still for about three seconds, as a person does who hears their name spoken in another room, then returned it to his hand and said "Again". The class meets in a former school in Wrocław that now holds the city''s reconstruction fund. Alex visited in January 2036, and she had set one condition in writing: no questions about the soldier. Her instrument store, once the school''s chemistry laboratory, holds about ninety instruments, each with a luggage label in her hand, and a rack of some thirty bows made by their players from cane, bamboo, a curtain rail, a ski pole, ash, hazel and green willow. In her own account she taught at the Academy in Warsaw, was twenty-eight when the city fell, and kept its basement open for four years. In a winter four years into the occupation a young soldier came into her studio at night, and she killed him. The carbon bow broke in the struggle and she left it behind. In the Zone she played most evenings in the old lion house at the Zoo, on an upturned feed bin, with a bow of stripped green willow strung with horsehair. Diana''s diary of 17 November 2034 records three of Catalina''s patients weeping at the violin, and Lucasz standing at the back.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/007', 'CHR', 'Catalina Chmielewska', 'Medic who runs the clinic. Aged 40 at first account, 41 later.', 'Before the war Catalina Chmielewska was a software engineer in Warsaw, at a firm that made scheduling systems for hospitals. After the invasion she trained as a volunteer medic. She came to the Zone from Kraków eighteen months before Diana met her at Brodno in the second week of October 2034. In the Zone she ran the old hospital at Brodno. In 2036 she runs a clinic in Kraków from a former bank, using the vault for stores. People called her selfless. Her rule for the dispensary was strict allocation by list. On one occasion there were forty courses of an antibiotic, allocated to the children, the post-operative and a man who had been shot. A Mrs Lewandowska, aged forty-six, with pneumonia in the Zoo, was not on the list. Her fourteen-year-old daughter, who had been stealing food from half the district to keep her alive, came to the hospital door at night and stood without knocking. Catalina did not let her in. In her own account Pawel, who kept the stores, took one course of the antibiotic to the girl at three in the morning and asked to be entered in the ledger as part of the number. At a hearing in a chapel Catalina said that her rule was wrong and that she and Pawel had been together for eight months, and the room answered that everyone knew. Mrs Lewandowska lived. Alisia''s account differs on one point: she says that Catalina came to the Zoo and looked at her mother. Catalina is not a doctor, and does not like to be called one in front of a patient. Diana found her at Brodno at forty, with a ledger in a green exercise book that set beside each stock count the number of people who would not receive the drug, which by her rule was nine a week. She contributed clinical notes to Protocol 003: of eighty-one exposed persons she treated, three did not become compliant, and each had been doing something for another person on the first day.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/008', 'CHR', 'Magda Wiśniewska', 'The Listener. Transcribed the Palace loudspeaker.', 'Magda Wiśniewska listens. She made the written transcript of the Palace loudspeaker broadcasts, morning and evening, and the archivist used her words unaltered. The four-second pause in the broadcast is on her page. Her transcript is the only record of the voice that a resident of Block C, Wiktor Szeliga, heard twice a day. She is called the Listener, a name she dislikes. Diana sat with her through the night of 21 to 22 September 2034 at OP-Rynek, in a long loft above the pharmacy with a sloping ceiling, a table of five radios, a lamp with a green shade and a notebook ruled in six columns. She was sixteen, thin and watchful, in a boy''s anorak, and she asked at the door whether Diana wanted tea before she decided whether she was welcome. At the hourly check-in of the five Russian posts she said that the third was lying, and that a man telling the truth takes his breath after he starts to speak. She judged the Polish woman''s voice on the northern recording to be a person asked to sound like a recording. Her transcripts are also the source of the words in Interlude III and Interlude VI, and her intercepts of the Russian net are rated 0.7 in Protocol 005. At eleven, in Mokotów, she gave the officers billeted in her house eleven names in return for chocolate and a pass, and four were taken to Szucha. She keeps the list. Alex met her in Lublin in March 2036, when she was eighteen.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/009', 'CHR', 'Zoysa Zieliński', 'Aged 17 at the first account and 19 later. Captain at the Dęblin air academy.', 'Zoysa was seventeen when she stood in the corner of Marek''s kitchen on a Thursday, boots tucked under her, back against the wall, with the bearing of a girl taught to stand to attention before she could read. When the story ended she stood, said "Thank you, Pan Marek", and left without her coat. Later, aged nineteen, she received the archivist at the air academy at Dęblin, precisely at ten to the hour, in an office with a map on one wall. She was reported to be the youngest officer of her rank in the Polish army, and wore the rank as a thing issued rather than chosen. The academy had four hundred and six students, aged fourteen to thirty-one, eleven aircraft that fly and nineteen that do not. They keep the nineteen because they teach more. During one period she was absent two nights; Goya said she was on patrol, but the roster showed no entry. Her mother was killed on the first night of the invasion, when Zoysa was eleven. Her father, Colonel Andrzej Zieliński, was reported killed holding a bridge on the Bug when she was thirteen. She came to the Zone at fifteen and asked for the Kosciuszko Group, and Goya took her to the range and found that she could shoot. In November 2034 Magda told her that a man with her father''s name and the rank of colonel had been heard on the Szucha net. On 2 December she crossed the river alone, without her coat, to a flat in Śródmieście, and found him alive and working for the occupiers. She did not shoot him. She made him her agent, and for seven months he gave the Brigade intelligence. She told Goya after the third month, and he did not speak to her for four days. She keeps a pad headed Order, a list of things in their place that includes the words Father, alive. In 2036 she visits her father in a flat in Lublin on Sundays.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/010', 'CHR', 'Alisia Lewandowska', 'Thief. Aged 14 when she stole to keep her mother alive, 16 when the archivist met her.', 'Alisia Lewandowska was fourteen when she stole food from half the district to keep her mother alive. The mother, Mrs Lewandowska, was forty-six and had pneumonia in the Zoo. When Alex met her in Warsaw in March 2036 she was sixteen, small and quick, in a sweatshirt and a lanyard, and within a minute she had taken his pen from the table. She gave it back. A girl of about fourteen in a too-large grey anorak was seen entering the keeper''s house at the Zoo by a side window with no catch. She took half a loaf, a bag of flour and a grey blanket in eleven seconds. Diana, who watched from the porch on 26 October 2034, knew her as Alisia and did not intervene. Nikita, aged six, laid a hand flat on the girl''s wrist, and Alisia put back the bread and flour and left by the front door. She grew up at Hel, where her father, a fisherman, was lost in the first flood on the coast when she was nine. She stole from the age of five and by twelve was the best thief in the district. Her mother recovered, and now makes jam in Gdynia. On the morning after the keeper''s house she went to the Brigade and confessed, and at fifteen she became its supply coordinator and kept its ledger. In 2036 she is the youngest investigator on a team of eleven at an anti-corruption foundation in Warsaw, and has twice visited Nikita in Wrocław.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/011', 'CHR', 'Anna', 'Elder of the two children at the keeper''s house, aged about 13.', 'Anna is perhaps thirteen. She and Nikita live in the keeper''s house at the south end of the Zoo, in the old reptile garden, a small brick house with a pitched roof and a tin chimney, now belonging to Pan and Pani Wróbel, who took in both children. Diana visited the house on 26 October 2034 with a message from Catalina, and waited on the porch while Anna read in the back room. A girl came in at a side window and took bread, flour and a blanket. Nikita laid a hand on her wrist, and the goods were returned. By February 2036 Anna is fifteen, tall, with a long plait, and she and Nikita live in a flat in Wrocław, on the second floor of a block near the river. She has been in charge of Nikita since she was thirteen. The day after their parents were taken she found Nikita in a cupboard, carried her down the stairs on her back and took her into the sewers, where they stayed three weeks. Nikita calls her Little Bird. She keeps the rules of the flat: the kitchen door half open, a rubber washer on the tap, a clock that does not tick. Nikita made it a condition of Alex''s visit that Anna be present. A fifth tap, five quick, was Diana''s own, and Nikita has not asked for it since. Fritha says that Anna would know whether Nikita still has the print she was given.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/012', 'CHR', 'Nikita Sokołowski', 'Aged 6. Does not speak. Hears what instruments do not.', 'Nikita was six in 2034, when Diana knew her, and eight in 2036. She does not speak. She is frightened of a single knock, so visitors knock twice and wait. She and Diana built a language eleven items long, which is the only record of their exchanges. She has been hearing, throughout, what the instruments pick up and the adults do not. Diana filed her drawing under MAG_REF_400, the mark for the organism itself, and copied it in her own hand. It was the first time she took a symbol from a child. Nikita''s own account, written for Alex in Wrocław in February 2036, says that she can speak and has decided not to. At six she heard five men on the stairs of their flat, whispered a warning to her father, and was put in a cupboard, and her parents did not return. She believed that the whisper had brought the men, and stopped making sound. Anna carried her into the sewers, where she heard patrols through the pipes and was the one who said stop. Diana first saw her at St Michael''s, beside Fritha, on 9 August 2034. At the keeper''s house she laid a hand flat on the wrist of a thief at the window. She hears a low note held under a cloth, and drew it as a circle with a wave in it and a cloth over the top. Diana judged her the most accurate instrument in the zone. The violin was the first sound that did not frighten her. The brief that Diana read on 28 December 2034 places her among the small proportion who resist the compound. In 2036 she paints in watercolours from a tin that Diana gave her, and cannot bear a ticking clock.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/013', 'CHR', 'Artur Wójcik', 'Boy of about 12 who works on the hand-built radio at Żerań.', 'At Żerań Diana found a hand-built radio assembled from boards, scavenged parts and hand-wound coils, mounted in a gutted instrument case, and a boy of about twelve working on its inside with a tool he had made himself. Artur reads what makes a machine stall. In Lucasz''s account, Artur worked out within about a week that the engineer had a fault that only one person could fix, and went to the Zoo to tell Tanja. He took the key from Lucasz''s belt for the lock-in, and on the pressure-fall night he ran the dock gauge reading to Diana. Lucasz places the lock-in on a Thursday in late October. Goya stood at the door and Lucasz was kept in the control room for seven hours, while Tanja played on the step outside. Diana knew of it and neither took part nor stopped it. In her field note at Żerań an unnamed boy, probably Artur, tells her that Lucasz does not sleep but switches off like a lamp and then switches on. The manuscript gives the boy''s age as about twelve at the arrival on 1 June 2034 and gives no surname.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/014', 'CHR', 'Pawel', 'Former banker, aged 35. Keeps the stores. Will not carry a weapon.', 'Pawel was a banker, thirty-five. He was in Warsaw because his father was in a nursing home in Praga and could not be moved, and when the water came he would not leave him. He stayed until the old man died, then stayed because he did not know where else to go. He keeps the stores and counts. He will not carry a weapon and that is the whole of his reputation. Men who had been at Mariupol called him a coward. Catalina did not, and says of him that he decided at some cost what he will not do. In the ward at Brodno, in Diana''s diary of 11 October 2034, Goya told him to count the blood on the floor, and he did. His ears went red and his pencil stopped, then began again. Catalina''s recording supplies the rest. They were together for eight months before it was known. He took one course of antibiotics from the dispensary at three in the morning, with the key she had left on a hook, and put it in the hand of Alisia Lewandowska at the Zoo for her mother, who was not on the list. He told Catalina in the morning and said she could put him in the number. At the hearing in the chapel she said the rule was wrong and that they were together, and the room answered that it already knew. In February 2036 they are still together in Kraków, where he does the accounts for her organisation. Diana put him in his mid-thirties in 2034. Alex, meeting him in Kraków, describes a thin man in his mid-forties in a good coat that has lost some of its edge.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/015', 'CHR', 'Marek Olszewski', 'Elder of the community. Tells stories on Thursdays.', 'Marek and Rosa are the two old people of the Zone, who passed through earlier wars as children and as parents. They keep a third-floor flat in Białołęka that stayed above the water. On Thursdays Marek tells stories at the kitchen table to whoever comes, Rosa makes tea and the children sit under the table, which is the warm place. Diana counted eleven present at one telling, on 30 November 2034, and chose not to record it, because some things are for being in the room. That night he told of a Home Army captain in the sewers under Śródmieście in August 1944, who kept a note offering terms in his coat for four days and never burned it. Marek said he had never decided what to think. Zoysa, in the corner by the stove, thanked him formally and left without her coat. In Protocol 005 he says, over tea with the children under the table, that the people of 1944 were killed by those who watched as well as by the Germans, and that he had waited ninety years for someone to say it aloud. Zoysa quotes his view that it does not matter whether the man in a story was a hero, only that the story was told.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/016', 'CHR', 'Rosa Olszewska', 'Elder who gave Janek a room.', 'Rosa shares the third-floor flat in Białołęka with Marek. In September she offered Janek the empty back room, saying it had been empty since the winter. She makes tea at Marek''s Thursday tellings. Janek had said he was only passing through, and she answered that he had been passing through for eleven years. He took the room in October and lived in it through the second winter. Diana, who had come with Marek to carry a table, asked him whether he could not have the room or did not think he was allowed. At the telling of 30 November 2034 Rosa puts her hand on Marek''s sleeve in the silence after the story. A Pani Olszewska, probably Rosa, speaks gently at the chapel hearing where Alisia Lewandowska confesses her thefts, and tells her that if she knows where everything is, she can tell them.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/017', 'CHR', 'The Runners', 'Couriers, mostly children. Also called the Ducks.', 'The Runners are mostly children who carry messages when the Russians jam the air, going where a boat would be seen and a man would be shot. Goya says they are called the Ducks, "whatever they''re calling them this year". They keep waypoints in the forest and use a winter route recorded in the Protocol 001 tables. A Runner of about thirteen was brought to the ward at Brodno by Goya with a bullet through the forearm and behaved as if it were a bee sting. Goya knows where each Runner in the wetlands is at any hour. A Runner stood at the church door when Diana first heard of Fritha and would not go in. A Runner first reported the hum of the Dendron to Diana, who did not at first believe her. On the night of the second flood Goya put twenty-three Runners out in four boats, the youngest nine, and counted twenty-three back. Iga, a Runner aged 23, was one of three lost at the Docks that night, having gone back for a boat. Runners brought the first reports of the third flood, including gunboats entering the canal at 04:00. A girl of about fifteen, Oliwia, carried Diana''s last item in her boot for fourteen months and got out.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/018', 'CHR', 'Alex, the archivist', 'Journalist who assembled the Protocols after the war.', 'Alex is the narrator of the archive. He is a journalist, sent into the Zone by the Guardian for a week. A postcard led him to a farm east of Warsaw, where Diana Antonoff had left her notebooks, binders, tapes and photographs with a note asking him to make them public. He then visited the speakers in Gdańsk, Berlin, Przemyśl, Wrocław, Kraków, Lublin, Dęblin and Warsaw (datelines November 2035 to March 2036) and set the book in Diana''s order. The five Protocols and the notes against each transcript are hers. Where he writes a reconstruction he puts it in italics and brackets, names the source of each passage, and says what he supplied himself. He met Diana once, for half an hour, at a farm in the grey zone in the last days before she went in. In each interlude he states that he never met the subject, builds one day in the subject''s voice from the surviving papers, and asks that the invented man not be taken for a witness.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/019', 'CHR', 'Wiktor Szeliga', 'Resident of Block C. Left no testimony.', 'Wiktor Szeliga gave no testimony and kept no diary. His traces are a pencil ration ledger kept by the block warden, Magda Wiśniewska''s transcript of the Palace loudspeaker, nine minutes of Diana''s tape and a neighbour''s account given in Lublin after the war. On the fifteenth he crossed the Vistula. The ledger records one adult male, ambulant, and one female child, nine, ambulant. A line for a non-ambulant person is ruled through, with no entry beside it. The neighbour said someone had gone up on the Thursday, that it was very quiet, that the room had been tidied and the bed made. He is a man who counts stairs. The child is Marta, his sister''s daughter, who came to him in June and has not spoken since the sixth day. Interlude VI, set on 14 June 2035, finds the two of them in Shelter 9, a former sports hall on the left bank, in beds 118 and 117. The ledger lists him as an orderly and has him on the potato detail for 302 mornings of 304. A gold ring on a shoelace was found in a cloakroom coat. On the last night the ledger records him awake and sitting up, and a second hand adds "Leave him". The following page is torn out, and the archivist does not know what became of him. Marta, grown, is a nurse in Gdańsk, and the only witness to anything he said.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/020', 'CHR', 'Sasha Lebedev', 'Russian operator at Observation Post R0. Relieved on 11 October.', 'Sasha Lebedev left no account. The archive holds eleven photocopied rota sheets, the post log in a green hardback notebook with a red spine, forty-four text messages from his mother recovered from a handset left in a drawer, a chocolate-bar wrapper and a receipt. On R0 he reads the sixth-day rota aloud to six field commanders, who answer "Received" and a number. He says "B" in place of the name of the authorising officer and keeps the same voice at the same speed throughout. He was relieved on 11 October. The log passes to a blocky, heavy hand. On the first page of that hand is the old instruction, "Do not add anything. Do not leave anything out. Do not pause", and under it in pencil, in another hand, "He paused." Protocol 002, section 7.3, gives him as a contracted operator and not a regular officer, aged 23 (E); in the reconstruction he gives the same age, from his mother''s messages. Survivors call him the Watcher, the boy on the radio (section 11.1). His room is four metres by three, with a steel shutter raised twenty centimetres. Through that strip he sees an eleven-storey block opposite whose lower four floors are covered by the green growth, and a man''s blue shirt on a sixth-floor balcony that has not moved in ninety-four days. The reconstructed day is 4 October. On 2 October the 06:00 transmission stops for four minutes and four seconds, and the log of the third records an equipment fault; the archivist says he does not know what he did in that time. A receipt for milk, sugar and a lighter, from a petrol station on the Radzymin road, is dated that afternoon. In Chapter 10, Magda Wiśniewska says she knew his voice from the net, and took the gap of 2 October for a message.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/021', 'CHR', 'Colonel Viktor Nikolaevich Sorokin', 'Commanding officer of the occupying force. Based at the Citadel.', 'Aged approximately 55 (E). His record, as far as it is known, is that of a professional: no excesses are attributed to him or to his staff in his name. He does not appear to hate the city. He appears to have orders and the competence to carry them out. It is reported that he reads. A source who delivered food to his office in July saw a clear desk with a single paperback and a glass of water. The title was not seen. Protocol 002 gives him first of three men, named in full with patronymic as they appear on the one set of orders recovered. It is Major Budanov''s name, and not the colonel''s, that appears on the orders. Survivors call the colonel the Iron One.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/022', 'CHR', 'Major Budanov', 'Head of screening and intelligence, Aleja Szucha. His office issues the rota. Referred to as "B".', 'Major Budanov''s office issues the sixth-day rota at 06:00. It lists the day''s movements by block, time, crew, task, duration, authorising officer and status. Each task is authorised "B". Operators read the order without saying the name. Protocol 002, section 7.2, gives his full name as Major Alexei Dmitrievich Budanov, aged approximately 44 (E), operating from Szucha. He is thorough, courteous to those brought in, and said to speak good Polish with an accent that recalls Kraków (C, two returned persons). He signs the letter D with a flourish. Survivors call him the Spaniel, for the way he hunts. Interlude III reconstructs one day at Aleja Szucha from a 212-page procedures binder, an appointments diary, a canteen card, a desk inventory and about three hundred of 4,100 numbered screening forms, found in eleven boxes in a basement in Praga. The archivist never met him and says the order of the day is his own. Each form ends in one of four words, and the last two are never explained on any form. One form records an interpreter who did not take the sweet offered at the fourth step, and Budanov notes in the margin that he is curious about her. Those who served under him would say only that he was always polite.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/023', 'CHR', 'Captain Isaev', 'Officer of the day at the Poniatowski Bridge checkpoint, aged 31.', 'Captain Isaev is thirty-one and sweats a little. He reports to Major Budanov at 08:40. He declines tea once, then accepts it. On the night in question there were 212 crossings. Of these, 190 were assessed on the bridge at Stage 2 or 3 and passed to the reception hall on the left bank. Twenty-two did not pass. He was asked what became of them and told the officer. He is the officer of the day at the checkpoint on the Poniatowski Bridge. Budanov receives him after reading fourteen overnight reports, each twice, once for fact and once for what is left out. The answer about the twenty-two is not given, and Isaev appears nowhere else in the manuscript. The manuscript gives no first name.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/024', 'CHR', 'Mrs Lewandowska', 'Alisia''s mother, aged 46, ill with pneumonia in the Zoo.', 'Mrs Lewandowska had pneumonia and was not on Catalina''s antibiotic list. Her daughter stole food from half the district to keep her alive. She was forty-six, and lay in a shed at the Zoo in the autumn of 2034. Catalina''s rule left nine people a week untreated, and the last forty courses of an antibiotic had been allocated without her. Alisia came to the hospital door at night and stood there without knocking. Pawel took one course from the dispensary and put it in Alisia''s hand. Mrs Lewandowska lived. In March 2036 Alisia says she is in Gdynia and makes jam. Diana''s note on Catalina''s transcript names her as the ninth, the number in the ledger that was never a count. Before the war the family lived at Hel, where her husband, a fisherman, drowned after taking a neighbour off a roof in the first flood. Alisia says his boat, the Marzena, was named for her mother.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/025', 'CHR', 'Pan and Pani Wróbel', 'Elderly couple who took in Anna and Nikita.', 'Pan and Pani Wróbel are an elderly couple who live in the keeper''s house at the south end of the Zoo and have taken in two children, Anna and Nikita. The house is a small brick building inside the old reptile garden, with a pitched roof and a tin chimney. One side window has no catch. On the afternoon of 26 October 2034 Pani Wróbel was out at the cistern, Pan Wróbel was asleep in a chair and Anna was reading in the back room, when Alisia Lewandowska took a half loaf, a bag of flour and a folded grey blanket from the shelf by the stove. Nikita laid a hand on her wrist, and Alisia put everything back. A visitor is told to knock twice and wait, because Nikita is frightened of a single knock. The house is also the setting of Diana''s diary of 4 November 2034 in Chapter 14, which does not name the couple.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/CHR/026', 'CHR', 'Mrs Kaczmarek', 'Old woman at the Zoo who leaves milk for the Dendron each morning and has stayed at stage 1 for three months.', 'Mrs Kaczmarek is an old woman at the Zoo. Her first name and her age are not given. She believes that the Dendron has been sent to teach people patience. Every morning at seven she leaves a saucer of milk at the foot of the largest mass. Diana records her in the notes to file of Protocol 003 as a person at stage 1 who has not passed to stage 2, and who has been at stage 1 for three months. Diana asked Catalina Chmielewska how that was possible. Catalina answered that the saucer is for someone else. Diana does not say what Catalina meant. In her diary of 28 December 2034, after she has read the brief, Diana writes of the woman at the Zoo who leaves milk for the organism and repeats the reason. She counts her among the forty thousand in the brief, as a datum, and does not give her name. Section 8 of Protocol 003 reports that the three persons who did not become compliant were each doing something for someone else on the first day. The manuscript does not connect Mrs Kaczmarek to them. It records no other speech or action by her and does not follow her beyond these two passages.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/OBJ/001', 'OBJ', 'Dendron', 'An organism of great age, recovered from permafrost, that spreads with floodwater and sedates those who stay near it.', 'The Dendron is the organism that spread through the Warsaw corridor with the flood. The name is a survivor''s name. Dendron is Greek for tree, and the organism is not a tree. It is reported to be of great age, recovered from permafrost in northern Siberia before the war, studied in Soviet laboratories for decades and made available for military use in the last years before the invasion. A Russian scientist who fled describes it as the first thing that was alive before anyone decided what alive meant. It climbs walls, pipes, railings and the iron of tram wires, and prefers damp, shade and the dark inside things. It clogs drains and sewers, so that the water rises and the organism grows in the new water. It makes a sustained tone at approximately 180 Hz with a slow beat of about six seconds, loudest at night and over still air. Those exposed to the compound do not appear to hear it. It emits a compound that is not a toxin and does not kill. It sedates. The effect is recorded in four stages, from unusual calm at stage 1 to obedience to any instruction at stage 4. In winter the visible growth retreats. Below 8 degrees Celsius the organism closes, and exposure is much less dangerous. Exposure is reduced by distance, by cold, by keeping upwind, and by filtration. It does not grow on the bricks of the Żerań power station, and no reason is known. The stem is the width of a finger and the colour of wet slate, and it has no leaves. Paired structures along it, about nine centimetres apart, show grey-white when open above 8 degrees Celsius. At the Żerań cooling channel a mature mass lowered the water level by 11 centimetres in nine days. In summer it is dense and hides. Wind is the main way exposure travels in the open, and the Filtry water station supplies the only water in the corridor reliably free of the compound. Catalina Chmielewska had treated eighty-one persons with documented exposure when the Protocol was compiled, and three of them did not become compliant. At the Zoo an old woman, Mrs Kaczmarek, leaves milk at the foot of the largest mass each morning at seven and has stayed at stage 1 for three months. The Russian word for what it does, mir, is also the word for peace. A hum with a six second beat is heard in Interlude IV and Interlude VI, and a sound of 180 hertz with the same beat is played beneath the interview table in Interlude III. The manuscript does not say that these are the Dendron''s tone.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/OBJ/002', 'OBJ', 'Dendron Dust', 'A pale powder scraped from the surface of mature Dendron growth.', 'The surface of a mature mass of the Dendron carries a pale powder in dry conditions. It can be scraped off. It is known in the corridor as Dendron Dust. In the dry months of summer the wind lifts it from the surface and carries it some distance, and it is then more widely spread than in any other season. Wet, it does not keep. Where it falls into standing water it dissolves, and the water that takes it is afterwards, in its effect, no different from water that carries the compound. Raw, it carries a trace of the compound, and breathed in on the wind it dulls as the water does. Prepared by those who gather it, scraped, dried and cut, and taken in the amounts used, it has the opposite effect. It is gathered in the cold months, when the organism is slow, and not in summer. Taken, the dust acts as a psychedelic. Members of the resistance use it in rituals.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/OBJ/003', 'OBJ', 'The Protocols (Diana''s bequest)', 'The collected papers and recordings Diana left, from which the archive is assembled.', 'The archivist found the body of Diana''s work beneath a canvas covering: three binders, a box of cassette tapes and some memory cards, a canvas roll of photographs and, laid across her notebooks where he would find it first, a manila folder with his name on it in her handwriting. The handwriting is deliberate, slanted forward, with the name underlined twice. Binder one is reproduced as found at the head of the relevant Protocols. Binder two holds annexes, among them Annex A, a list of forty-one streets given with both their present and their pre-1944 names, which the survivors use. The tapes carry the recorded testimonies. The four notebooks were wrapped separately in oilcloth and laid on top. The single sheet in the folder tells Alex to make the Protocols public. Diana counts forty cassettes by 2 September 2034. The Foreword speaks of five Protocols. Chapters 3, 6, 9, 12 and 15 each open with one, marked as binder one and reproduced as found, and Protocol 004 records that binder one holds Protocols 001 to 003 and itself. Binder two holds the crate label, a page giving the name of the programme, the initial of the Russian scientist and the brief in full. The binder of Budanov''s procedures in Interlude III is a different object. Alex says that Protocol 005 reached him by another route, in a pouch kept in a Runner''s boot for fourteen months.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/OBJ/004', 'OBJ', 'Appendix: The Brief', 'The document that names the programme behind the Dendron.', 'The Brief is the document that Diana received on 28 December 2034, which Protocol 005 describes in part and which Alex reproduces in full in the appendix of the manuscript, from the second binder. It records Operation Neural Harvest, its origins, the parties that supplied the organism and the science, and its stated aim, population compliance optimisation, set down without comment. It is eleven pages, in Russian and in English, on the stationery of a research directorate, stamped, numbered and signed with initials. Diana does not copy the names of the Western partners, on the ground that a binder can be found. In the appendix each place where a partner name stood is marked withheld, and the initials of the signatories are not copied either. The brief is marked Restricted and copy 3 of 5, gives ethical review as not applicable, and is distributed to the Directorate, the Operator and three partner establishments. The brief describes the survivors, about forty thousand, as a test cohort, the flood as a delivery system, the stages of the organism''s effect as behavioural endpoints, and stage 4 as the target state. It gives the city''s qualities as isolated, contained and non-attributable. It holds the results in a joint repository, to be shared with partners on completion of the cohort phase, sets a target of not less than 60 per cent of the cohort at stage 3 or above within 90 days of continuous exposure, and expects a small proportion of the population to show atypical resistance. Four annexes, A to D, are listed in it and are not reproduced. Diana reads it four times on 28 December 2034 and finds the tidiness of it the worst part. In Interlude V a brief of matching wording is on slide four of a deck. The manuscript does not say that it is the same document.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/OBJ/005', 'OBJ', 'Janek''s prayer book', 'Taped prayer book, one of Janek''s three possessions.', 'Janek owns a coat, a pair of boots and a prayer book. The spine of the book has been taped many times, and the older layers of tape show yellow under the new. He let Diana hold it for about a minute, on the dock at Żerań on 19 August 2034. On another occasion he put it on the oilcloth between them, spine up, in the course of a conversation. The occasion was Alex''s visit to Janek in Przemyśl in January 2036. Janek told him that Diana had held the book for about a minute, and that it was the first time anyone had held it who did not want it to be anything. Alex asked to photograph it, and Janek refused. Janek studied for the priesthood and left it to marry, and his wife died of cancer five years before the invasion. Goya, in Chapter 1, remembers him as a man who owned nothing in the world but a coat and a book.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/OBJ/006', 'OBJ', 'Fritha''s prints', 'Forty-one family photographs recovered from the flood.', 'Forty-one prints were carried in her father''s jacket, which Fritha found on a railing six days after the flood. They were dried on a radiator in a stairwell without power. The emulsion had lifted and her mother''s face had shifted a little to the left. Her family had gone back for eleven albums in the forty minutes after the dam went, and the prints are what she took out of the water. She dried them in front of a stove made from a bucket. Her father was a photographer with a studio in Praga, and her mother worked in conservation at the National Museum. The forty-one began a larger collection. By 9 August 2034 Diana found several thousand photographs in rows on the altar steps of St Michael''s in Praga, each weighted at the corners with a pebble or a spent cartridge and sorted by year.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOR/001', 'LOR', 'Operation Neural Harvest', 'The Russian research programme under which the surviving population of Warsaw was held as a test cohort.', 'Neural Harvest is the name of a research programme described in an operational brief that Diana Antonoff received on 28 December, through the Russian scientist she calls a source in Protocol 003. The brief is eleven pages, in Russian and in English, on the stationery of a research directorate, stamped and numbered. Hers was copy 3 of 5. The brief treats the survivors of the Warsaw corridor, estimated at forty thousand, as a test cohort. It treats the flood as the delivery system. It records the effects of the organism as behavioural endpoints in four stages and names stage 4 as the target state. The brief names loss of containment as the principal risk to the programme, and loss of the measurement through the emergence of an organised population as the secondary risk. It considers both low. A small proportion of the cohort is expected to show atypical resistance. Such subjects are to be identified, monitored and recorded. They are not to be removed from the cohort unless they affect the integrity of the measurement. The target for the cohort phase is that not less than 60 per cent of the cohort is assessed at stage 3 or above within 90 days of the establishment of continuous exposure. The phase ends when the Directorate determines that the measurement is complete. Results are held in a joint repository and are to be shared with the partner establishments on completion of the cohort phase. The brief gives ethical review as not applicable and states no reason. The name comes from the observation section: a harvest is the gathering of what has grown. The brief is reproduced as Appendix: The Brief, with the names of the Western partners and the initials withheld. It lists four annexes: A, the organism; B, delivery; C, behavioural endpoints; and D, observation, cordon and data. They are in the binder and are not reproduced. The programme is described as joint, run by the Russian state and at least two Western states through their agencies, military research establishments and private biotechnology contractors. The Russians supplied the organism and the Western partners supplied the means of enhancing it, stabilising it in a water supply and optimising it. The brief gives the grounds for the choice of city as isolated, contained and non-attributable, with no external access and no prospect of relief. The scientist gave Diana the name on 30 October 2034, and she wrote it in the second binder, not in Protocol 003. She believes the cohort phase ended with the destruction of Żerań on 1 January 2035, and rates that belief 0.6. In Interlude V an account lead works on a contract called the Programme, whose name has two words, one suggesting the brain and one a verb for gathering a crop.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOR/002', 'LOR', 'The stages of compliance', 'The occupiers'' scale for assessing how far a person has been pacified.', 'The occupiers assess residents on a numbered scale. Stage 3 is the point at which a person will give up a name when asked. Stage 4 is the point at which they will give up one without being asked. Stage 5 is reported to the operator at R0 as not his concern. The district dashboard shows a cohort status, a percentage of the district assessed at Stage 3 or above, which stood at 71.2 per cent on the day recorded. The rota sends crews to collect persons assessed at Stage 4 who are non-ambulant. At the Poniatowski Bridge checkpoint, arrivals are assessed on the bridge at Stage 2 or 3 and passed to a reception hall. The scale is not the same as the medical staging of pressure sores, which one carer also records as Stage 2 and then Stage 4. Two accounts of the stages exist. Catalina Chmielewska''s clinical table in Protocol 003 gives four, by time of continuous exposure, from unusual calm within four hours at stage 1 to compliance after four days at stage 4, where a person follows any instruction without hostility. The occupiers read the same stages by behaviour. In Interlude III, Budanov records that at stage 1 a subject refuses the water, at stage 2 drinks it quickly with a tremor, at stage 3 drinks it steadily and holds the glass in both hands, and at stage 4 neither drinks nor declines. A stage 0 appears in the screening records and on the dashboard in Interlude V, where a person at stage 0 is noted as resistant. The brief described in Protocol 005 names stage 4 as the target state. Protocol 005 also uses the word stage for the three phases of the destruction of Żerań, which is unrelated.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOR/003', 'LOR', 'The Warsaw Uprising of 1944', 'The historical parallel the survivors and Diana draw on.', 'Marek Olszewski gave the following account. In 1944 the Home Army rose against the German occupation and fought for sixty-three days. It was destroyed. During those days the Red Army stood on the east bank of the Vistula, within sight of the city, and did not cross. When the rising failed, the German command razed what remained of the city, block by block, with explosives and fire, as punishment. Of the city on the left bank, eighty-five per cent was destroyed. The Old Town and Royal Castle were rebuilt afterwards from photographs and paintings. Marek also told the story of a Home Army captain in the sewers under Śródmieście in August: a hard man who held his line for three weeks because his men believed he would never leave them. In September the men found a note in his coat offering him terms. He had not accepted it, and had not burned it either. He kept it four days. Marek''s judgement was that the men could not forgive that he was a good man who had kept the door open. Diana describes the destruction of Żerań in terms of 1944, and says the comparison was given to her and she declined to refuse it. Marek told Diana the account over tea, in a kitchen with the children under the table, and said he had spent ninety years waiting for someone to say it aloud. He told the story of the captain on Thursday 30 November 2034, to eleven people in his and Rosa''s flat in Białołęka. Zoysa Zieliński was in the room and thanked him formally before leaving without her coat. Diana sets 1944 beside Żerań in a table of six rows and does not claim that the two are the same, only that the author of the second knew the first. Protocol 002 records the Old Town as occupied for its symbolism, with loudspeakers on the ramparts.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOR/004', 'LOR', 'Street names', 'Survivors navigate by street names that were withdrawn before 1944.', 'Many streets in the flooded corridor are passable only under names that the municipal authority withdrew before 1944. Those are the names in use among the survivors and the ones that will be understood. A list of forty-one streets, with both names, is given in Annex A, which is held in binder two and not reproduced. The Foreword describes the Protocols as listing streets that no longer existed under names unused since 1944. The Protocol gives no individual street name, and no other passage in the manuscript gives one, so the list is not available to the reader.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOR/005', 'LOR', 'Rising water', 'Why the flood does not recede.', 'The water has not receded because the causes have not been corrected. Reservoirs are damaged and cannot absorb rainfall. The dam at Włocławek is non-functional. Breached dikes cannot be repaired under occupation. Pumping stations have no power. The occupiers prioritise their own routes and show no interest in civilian drainage (E). The water plateaus and does not pass. Protocol 001 gives the causes as a cascade of five failures upstream between 11 and 16 October 2028, at Solina, Czorsztyn, Sandomierz, Włocławek and the Narew retention works. It puts the corridor at 84 m, plus or minus 0.4 m with rainfall, against a pre-flood datum of 77 m, read daily at the Żerań dock gauge. The explanation is graded E, and the manuscript shows the occupiers doing more than neglect the water. Lucasz Kowalski, who wrote the gate sequence of the Vistula Canal, says that those who took the control room swapped two lines, so that the canal brings water in. The third flood, on the night of 31 December 2034, is recorded as an act. The gates were opened in sequence with the lower gates held shut, and the Żerań gauge rose from 84.3 m at 21:00 to 88.6 m at 02:05.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
insert into public.entries (ref, type, name, summary, entry) values ('SYNPTCL/LOR/006', 'LOR', 'Screening', 'The occupiers'' seven step procedure for establishing the stage of each subject, as run by Major Budanov at Aleja Szucha.', 'Screening is the procedure by which the occupiers establish the stage of each person brought to them. Protocol 002 names the screening camps and their head, Major Budanov, and says that what the screening consists of is not known to Diana. Interlude III gives the procedure. It is seven steps, on pages 61 to 88 of a ring-bound procedures binder of 212 pages, in Russian with a Polish translation. A committee wrote it. Budanov revised it in his first week, to take out what was unnecessary, and the word distress in its opening sentence is his. That sentence gives the purpose: to establish, in the shortest time and with the least distress, the stage of each subject. The steps are reception, observation, baseline, the offer, questions, recording and disposition. In observation the subject is watched in silence for between ninety seconds and three minutes, and the stage is read from what the subject does with a glass of water. At stage 1 the subject refuses it. At stage 2 the subject drinks it quickly, with a tremor. At stage 3 the subject drinks it steadily and holds the glass in both hands. At stage 4 the subject neither drinks nor declines. The baseline is five questions, on name, address, date of birth, occupation and nearest relations, with the delay before each answer noted in seconds. In the offer a boiled sweet is held out. About half of the subjects at stage 3 take it, and all at stage 4. The questions then ask for the names of persons hostile to the order of the district, without a raised voice or any touch. A clerk behind glass transcribes the answers in triplicate. The interviewer writes the stage and one of four decisions: Release, Retain, Transfer or Treat. The last two are not explained on any form that Alex has read. A one page annex to the binder, headed Acoustic Conditioning and unsigned, gives a low sound of about 180 hertz with a beat of six seconds, to be played beneath the table for a subject in the first days of exposure. It is not one of the seven steps, and the subject is not told of it. The screening record runs to 4,100 numbered forms, found in eleven boxes in a basement in Praga. Alex has read about three hundred. On one form the box for the sweet is blank. The subject is an interpreter, a woman of forty-one, assessed at stage 0, and the margin carries a note in Budanov''s hand that she did not take it and that he is curious about her. The room, the lamp and the manner of the interviewer in the reconstruction are Alex''s own and are not tagged to a source.') on conflict (ref) do nothing;
